<%# Every labelled control in the app renders through here, so the label, the
    error message and the wiring between them are written once (§7) rather than
    per form and half-remembered.

    aria-describedby is that wiring, and it is the part that is easy to skip:
    without it a screen reader announces "Title, edit text, invalid" and never
    reaches the sentence saying what was actually wrong. It is emitted only when
    there is a message to point at — a describedby aimed at an element that was
    never rendered is a dangling reference, which some readers announce as
    nothing at all.

    form.object is nil on the sign-in and password forms, which post to a URL
    rather than to a model. Those still get the label and the focus ring; they
    simply have no per-field errors to wire up. %>
<% messages = form.object&.errors&.[](attribute) || [] %>
<% error_id = "#{form.object_name}_#{attribute}_error" %>
<% attributes = { class: field_classes(invalid: messages.any?) } %>
<% attributes.merge!("aria-describedby": error_id, "aria-invalid": "true") if messages.any? %>

<div>
  <%= form.label attribute, local_assigns[:label], class: "block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900" %>
  <div class="mt-1">
    <%= yield attributes %>
  </div>
  <% if messages.any? %>
    <p id="<%= error_id %>" class="mt-1 text-sm text-red-700"><%= messages.to_sentence.upcase_first %>.</p>
  <% end %>
</div>
